@SBK in TO 
Again, this simply misunderstands the difference between ñ and n. It would be like using an l (L) instead of a t because "it's only missing a little line across it". Sorry, but it's a different letter and a different word if you use it wrongly. In the case of 'espanol' it's a word that isn't in any dictionary, English or Spanish. Yes it's "recognizable", but that's a ridiculously low bar and just serves to reinforce ignorance about other languages, definitely not building bridges or fostering any mutual understanding as some seem to oddly believe. They've finally retired "ano" for año apparently (thankfully), time to take the next logical step and not include any mistaken ñ spellings that aren't even English words.
And llama is fine, it's just a double l. More like a 'th' combination in English that alerts them pronunciation of the letters.You won't find an ll key on a Spanish keyboard, but you will certainly find an ñ key.

@Alex 

It’s a convention. Everyone tacitly understands it’s incorrect but it allows for more words to be used in crosswords which is an admirable goal. 

Think about it this way, what about other languages with a different alphabet entirely? AGORA, SHALOM, SAYONARA, obviously these are transliterations. I think expanding language and culture is always a better choice in crosswords than not.

@Lazarus Long 
Nobody said that Confucianism and Taoism are the same thing, the clue merely states that it is a Confucian principle. "Tao" means “the Way” or “the Path". In Confucianism the Tao is the proper way to live and conduct oneself, emphasizing moral cultivation, virtue, appropriate relationships, and social harmony.
Confucius frequently spoke of the Way in the Analects.

